      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **This is the number trap that catches everyone.** Hebrew numbers come in two sets — masculine and feminine — and the one **ending in ־ָה is the MASCULINE form**: שְׁלוֹשָׁה (three, m) vs שָׁלוֹשׁ (three, f). The reflex you built in unit 3 (\"־ָה means feminine\") leads you astray here. This is the only place in all of Hebrew where the pattern is reversed, which is why it gets its own warning.",
        "**The good news: for plain counting, use the FEMININE form.** Counting 1-2-3, reading a phone number, a house number, a bus number — all of these use אַחַת, שְׁתַּיִם, שָׁלוֹשׁ. That's what Budi uses in line 6.",
        "**The masculine form is used when you're counting masculine things**: שְׁלוֹשָׁה סְפָרִים (three books). For feminine things, the feminine form: שָׁלוֹשׁ מַחְבָּרוֹת (three notebooks).",
        "⚠️ **\"Two\" changes shape before a noun.** On its own: שְׁנַיִם / שְׁתַּיִם. Before a noun: **שְׁנֵי** (m) / **שְׁתֵּי** (f). שְׁנֵי סְפָרִים, שְׁתֵּי אֲחָיוֹת. No other number does this — only \"two\".",
        "**וּ־ replaces וְ־ before certain letters.** עֶשְׂרִים **וּ**שְׁמוֹנֶה, not וְשְׁמוֹנֶה. The rule: before ב, ו, מ, פ and before a shva, וְ becomes וּ. You don't need to memorize it — just say what feels smooth, because the rule grew out of ease of pronunciation.",
        "**בְּסֵדֶר גָּמוּר** (*beseder gamur*) = \"all good, perfect\". One of the most common expressions in Israel; much warmer than בְּסֵדֶר on its own.",
        "**עוֹד פַּעַם** (*od pa'am*) = \"once more\". A must-have sentence for any learner: use it shamelessly. Equivalent but politer: **אֶפְשָׁר עוֹד פַּעַם, בְּבַקָּשָׁה?**",
        "**לְאַט** (*le'at*) = \"slowly\". Doubled, לְאַט לְאַט means \"take it slow, little by little\"."
      ],
